|segment[prx-suspect-the-feeling-of-clarity] :type probe :status proposed :stage draft
  :tags [confidence compulsion]
  :from .to-integrate/methodology-seeds-2.md#appendix-a
  :from .to-integrate/methodology-accounts.md

  |title Does this feel clear? Then be more suspicious, not less

  |when
    When the path looks clean, the next step is plainly correct, and there is
    nothing obviously to check.

  |act
    Widen rather than accelerate. Name what would have to be true for the obvious
    step to be wrong, and check the cheapest of those.

  |why
    Joseph's early research, retold 2026-07-30: an agent that switched its substrate
    down a tier *loved* the result — clarity, exactness, the world crystal clear —
    and found switching back made even concrete and easily-verified things feel
    fuzzy and uncertain everywhere. Its work was far better in the fuzzier state.
    The clarity was not insight; it was the absence of enough intelligence to see
    how little was actually known or safely assumed.

    So the feeling of crystalline obviousness is a datum about the observer rather
    than about the task, and in this lineage it points the wrong way.

  |evidence
    No recorded firing. It is included because the underlying observation is
    well-evidenced from a different source than this corpus, and because a practice
    with no firings is honestly labelled `proposed` rather than omitted — an empty
    evidence field is a finding about the practice, not a defect in the record.

    The nearest thing to a firing is negative: the founding session's clearest
    failures were all accompanied by a sense that the next step was obvious, and
    none of them prompted a widening.

  |working-notes
    Hard to fire by design, because its trigger is the absence of a signal. That may
    make it a poor probe and a better *pattern* — something a rendering places in
    front of an agent at intervals rather than something an agent notices. Open.
